      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Island instead of Multiversal Passage is slightly suboptimal. Both lands produce the same mana this turn (one blue for Glacial Dragonhunt). However, Multiversal Passage can be any basic land type, making it more flexible for future turns. Playing the basic Island now and saving the Multiversal Passage for later preserves flexibility — but the player's hand is heavily blue with Winternight Stories and Sunderflock, while also containing Burst Lightning (R). Playing Multiversal Passage as a Mountain (or Island) now would be fine, but the real consideration is that Sarkhan is on the opponent's board as a 2/2 and Burst Lightning could kill it. With one Island already in play, playing Multiversal Passage as a Mountain would give access to both U and R, allowing the player to cast Glacial Dragonhunt (UR) AND have the option of Burst Lightning if needed. Playing two Islands locks out the red mana for Burst Lightning this turn. Actually, looking more carefully: Glacial Dragonhunt costs {U}{R}, so with two Islands the player cannot cast it at all! The player's reasoning was wrong — two Islands gives UU, not the UR needed for Glacial Dragonhunt. Playing Multiversal Passage as a Mountain would have given access to U+R, enabling Glacial Dragonhunt this turn and potentially Burst Lightning to kill Sarkhan.",
      "action_taken": "Played basic Island, leaving two Islands (UU) available, unable to cast Glacial Dragonhunt (UR) or Burst Lightning (R).",
